    <p class="note insight">Needs for safety, respect, and rest are human. Guilt about having needs is often trained, not innate. You are allowed to take up space.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h2>How a pattern can read</h2>
    <p>This file no longer calculates a score. These are the four readings the questions were written around. They are a mirror, not a verdict.</p>
    <div class="step">
<b>Fewer patterns showing up</b> A lighter load is good news. Stay kind to yourself either way.<br>Based on what you shared, many of the more intense narcissistic patterns don’t appear frequent. That can be reassuring — and this result is still only a mirror of your answers, not a diagnosis of anyone.<div class="step">Keep trusting your gut when something feels off — low scores don’t erase single harmful incidents.</div>
<div class="step">Healthy relationships still need boundaries, repair, and mutual respect.</div>
<div class="step">If something still feels wrong, you can use Ground tools or talk with a trusted person or therapist.</div>
</div>
<div class="step">
<b>Some patterns worth noticing</b> Clarity is strength. You don’t need certainty to take care of yourself.<br>You’re noticing behaviours that can show up in narcissistic dynamics — not always, but often enough to matter. Patterns matter more than labels. What you’re feeling is information, not overreacting.<div class="step">Name specific behaviours rather than trying to prove a label.</div>
<div class="step">Practice small, clear boundaries and notice how they respond over time.</div>
<div class="step">Lean on grounding tools when interactions leave you shaky or confused.</div>
<div class="step">Consider journaling incidents — dates, what was said, how you felt — to keep your reality steady.</div>
</div>
<div class="step">
<b>Several patterns appear often</b> You’re not broken for being affected. You’re human — and you’re allowed to protect your peace.<br>Your answers suggest frequent experiences that align with narcissistic abuse dynamics — gaslighting, control, devaluation, or one-way empathy. This is not a clinical diagnosis; it is validation that what you’ve lived may be seriously harmful.<div class="step">Prioritize safety and emotional recovery over winning arguments or changing them.</div>
<div class="step">Build support outside the relationship — friends, family, a counsellor, or a hotline.</div>
<div class="step">Use the Safety section to plan contacts and exits if you feel unsafe.</div>
<div class="step">Boundaries may be met with pushback; that reaction is data, not proof you were wrong.</div>
</div>
<div class="step">
<b>Strong, recurring patterns</b> You are not alone, and you are not “too much.” Support exists, and strength includes asking for it.<br>What you described points to a high frequency of behaviours associated with narcissistic abuse and significant emotional impact. Please hear this gently: your experiences matter, and you deserve safety and support. This is not a diagnosis of your partner — it is a compassionate reading of your answers.<div class="step">If you feel unsafe, contact local emergency services or a domestic violence hotline (see Help).</div>
<div class="step">Consider a safety plan before big conversations, separations, or moves.</div>
<div class="step">You do not have to confront them or get them to agree in order to heal.</div>
<div class="step">Leaving is one option; so are boundaries and staged exits — only you know your situation.</div>
</div>
    <p class="note">Not a medical or legal diagnosis. Only a qualified professional can assess personality disorders.</p>
  </div>
</div>

